  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nModels are in agreement keeping the high pressure anchored over the \nDesert Southwest through this week and into next week. It's a tough \npattern to break, and looks to keep the heat on and dry air in place \nfor the foreseeable future. August has come in like a lion setting \nnew record highs for the Grand Valley each of the last three days, \nand it looks like this trend will continue through the weekend. The \nensembles were looking to shift the high to the east over Oklahoma \nthe other day, opening the door to the moisture over Mexico to move \nnorth, but are now backing off that scenario 'til late next week. \nIt's just hard to say this far out. We'll just have to see how this \nplays out. A Heat Advisory is still in effect for tomorrow for \nsoutheastern Utah extending east into the Grand Valley for the \ncontinued triple digit temperatures though they do cool a couple \ndegrees from Monday's highs as a trough passes to the north to \nsquash the high a bit, and smoke from the Utah fires will reduce the \nsolar insolation. Temperatures look to cool yet another couple \ndegrees Wednesday before warming back up as the high to the \nsouthwest builds in over eastern Utah and Western Colorado. The \ntrough to the north dipping south increases the winds aloft that \nwill mix down through the afternoon, and with the dry air in place \nacross the region and the hot temp driving the relative humidity \ninto the single digits, Red Flag Warnings are in effect for Fire \nWeather zones 201 (Rio Blanco and southern Moffat Counties), 205 \n(Flat Tops and Grand Mesa), and 207 (the Four Corners in \nsouthwestern Colorado) from 1 PM to 7 PM. There are indications that \nthe Colorado mountains may see continued critical fire weather \nconditions Wednesday and Thursday, but the forecast winds are barely \nmeeting criteria. Held off on any watches for these areas to see how \nthis develops. If the high does shift up over the region, winds will \ndrop off. Stay tuned on this. Even with the winds dropping off, with \nthe hot temperatures and very dry conditions in place, there is \nstill increased fire risks. If you're out in the mountains, exercise \nextra care to prevent any fire starts.",
